  },
  {
   "id": 615704,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "During the second half of the nineteenth century, which of the following was the most common outcome of events like those described in the excerpt?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1170784,
     "content_html": "Labor disputes ended with small gains for laborers."
    },
    {
     "id": 1170783,
     "content_html": "Most strikes had a significantly negative outcome for workers."
    },
    {
     "id": 1170785,
     "content_html": "Violence led to major losses for labor union members."
    },
    {
     "id": 1170782,
     "content_html": "Labor unions grew larger after each significant dispute."
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 615705,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "According to the excerpt, the union at the Homestead plant believed Frick would be unsuccessful in breaking their strike because",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1170788,
     "content_html": "he lacked resolve and would lose his zeal for holding out against the strikers"
    },
    {
     "id": 1170789,
     "content_html": "the workers at Homestead would be harder to defeat and replace than those in the coke fields had been"
    },
    {
     "id": 1170787,
     "content_html": "the workers at Homestead were more numerous than those in the coke fields"
    },
    {
     "id": 1170786,
     "content_html": "Carnegie would influence Frick to seek a compromise settlement that was fair to the workers by meeting some of their demands"
    }
